  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nWednesday: An area of mid level low pressure will move east \nacross the northern Great Lakes with a cold front approaching \nthe area from the northwest. In the upper levels of the \natmosphere a dual jet streak will elongate over Iowa, Missouri,\nIllinois, and Indiana. The first jet streak appears to be \nassociated with the long wave trough axis centered across the \ncentral United States, while the second jet streak is rounding \nthe top of an amplifying ridge over the Rocky Mountains. This is\nan interesting setup, as forecast soundings do show some \ncapping in place with the primary forcing for ascent well \nnortheast of the area. However, the surface boundary coupled \nwith the 250 mb jet streak might provide just enough lift for \nthunderstorms. The latest run of the NBM has 10 - 20% PoPs \nacross the area, with LREF PoPs remain consistently higher (50 -\n70%). Limiting the QPF on the LREF to 0.05\" though reduces the \nPoPs to 30 - 40%). Looking at forecast soundings, there is not \nonly weak capping in place but a skinnier CAPE. This makes the \nlower QPF footprint understandable from a ensemble perspective \n(most guidance likely not initiating thunderstorms while some \nare). Ahead of the cold front, CAPE values rise to around 2000 \nJ/kg, with SFC to 6 km shear values around 30 kt. If updrafts \nbecome robust enough to penetrate the capping inversion and \ndrier air, locally damage wind gusts and severe hail could be \npossible. As such, SPC has our far northeastern zones in a \nmarginal risk of severe weather. \n\nThursday - Sunday: The surface cold front from Wednesday will clear \nMissouri and Illinois with seasonable temperatures and \ndewpoints expected through Friday (no precipitation is \nforecast). By Friday, the anticyclonic flow that was over the \nRocky Mountains will start to nudge east. As such, temperatures \nare expected to warm back up into the 90s for Saturday and \nSunday. Spread on both the LREF and NBM is around 5 degrees with\nthe WPC Cluster Analysis showing a good amount of dispersion \nbetween the GEFS, GEPS, and EPS. All of this being said, \nprecipitation is generally not expected Friday through Sunday \nwith a gradual warming trend forecast.",
